body:
> Perhaps an alternative solution to this PR would be to implement "greater than" and "less than" operations between String & StringName?

That wouldn't solve this problem on its own

I'd say that the alternative here is to change:
```cpp
struct _ArrayVariantSort {
	_FORCE_INLINE_ bool operator()(const Variant &p_l, const Variant &p_r) const {
		bool valid = false;
		Variant res;
		Variant::evaluate(Variant::OP_LESS, p_l, p_r, res, valid);
		if (!valid) {
			res = false;
		}
		return res;
	}
};
```
Into:
```cpp
struct _ArrayVariantSort {
	_FORCE_INLINE_ bool operator()(const Variant &p_l, const Variant &p_r) const {
		return p_l < p_r;
	}
};
```
Or just use `Comparator<Variant>` directly

This will replicate what I suspect is the intended behavior, sorting by type then by value, which is how sorting `Vector` will sort (unless I'm mistaken)

And the same for `Dictionary`, or possibly just the changes in this PR

Sorting `float` and `int` together isn't done *anywhere*, but it would be less confusing, but that would require a new separate ordering check as `StringLikeVariantOrder` doesn't sort it like that *either*, no current sorting method sorts this way (using `Variant::evaluate` *does* do that for `float/int` specifically, but not `Variant::operator<`, but `Variant::evaluate` still breaks with *other* non-matching types)

I don't think we should rely on C++20 for this, and it would be a far more drastic change to change how variant comparison works in *general* and the impact of that would have to be evaluated, for example given how `Vector<Variant>` sorts by type and then value

The other solution here would be:
```cpp
struct _ArrayVariantSort {
	_FORCE_INLINE_ bool operator()(const Variant &p_l, const Variant &p_r) const {
		bool valid = false;
		Variant res;
		Variant::evaluate(Variant::OP_LESS, p_l, p_r, res, valid);
		if (!valid) {
			res = p_l.get_type() < p_r.get_type();
		}
		return res;
	}
};
```
And add comparison between `String` and `StringName` to operations, but that'd be quite a drastic change on the side of `Variant`

I'd advocate for a more localized fix for this, by creating a new comparison method that compares both `String` and `StringName`, a well as `float` and `int`, and then otherwise sorts by type, that would be the least confusing IMO, so something like:
```cpp
bool ArrayVariantOrder::compare(const Variant &p_lhs, const Variant &p_rhs) {
	if (p_lhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
		const String &lhs = *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_lhs);
		if (p_rhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
			return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_rhs));
		} else if (p_rhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
			return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_rhs));
		}
	} else if (p_lhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
		const StringName &lhs = *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_lhs);
		if (p_rhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
			return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_rhs));
		} else if (p_rhs.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
			return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_rhs));
		}
	} else if (p_lhs.get_type() == Variant::FLOAT) {
		...
	} else if (p_lhs.get_type() == Variant::INT) {
		...
	}

	return p_lhs < p_rhs;
}
```

We could alternatively change `Variant::operator<` to be:
```cpp
bool Variant::operator<(const Variant &p_variant) const {
	bool v;
	Variant r;
	evaluate(OP_LESS, *this, p_variant, r, v);
	if (v) {
		return r;
	}
	return type < p_variant.type; // If no comparator exists, order by type.
}
```

With some testing I've found that this seems the most complete and direct solution, as a new comparison method:
```cpp
if (p_left.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
	const String &lhs = *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_left);
	if (p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_right));
	} else if (p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_right));
	}
} else if (p_left.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
	const StringName &lhs = *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_left);
	if (p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_right));
	} else if (p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(lhs, *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_right));
	}
}

bool valid = false;
Variant res;
Variant::evaluate(Variant::OP_LESS, p_left, p_right, res, valid);
if (!valid) {
	res = p_left.get_type() < p_right.get_type();
}
return res;
```

This will:
* Sort `String` and `StringName` together, alphabetically
* Sort `float` and `int` together
* Sort other cases with matching types as normal (i.e. where there's a comparison available)
* Sort other cases by type

This could simply replace `StringLikeVariantOrder`, the only thing it does differently is that it doesn't check matching types first like `Variant::operator<` but instead only checks that when a comparison is lacking or otherwise invalid, alternatively we can keep `StringLikeVariantOrder` as is and change `Variant::operator<` to do the same, but that'd be a bit more risky

Alternative (untested) solution that leverages the performance of `Variant::evaluate`
```cpp
bool valid = false;
Variant res;
Variant::evaluate(Variant::OP_LESS, p_left, p_right, res, valid);
if (!valid) {
	if (p_left.get_type() == Variant::STRING && p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(*VariantInternal::get_string(&p_left), *VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_right));
	} else if (p_left.get_type() == Variant::STRING_NAME && p_right.get_type() == Variant::STRING) {
		return StringName::AlphCompare::compare(*VariantInternal::get_string_name(&p_left), *VariantInternal::get_string(&p_right));
	}
	return p_left.get_type() < p_right.get_type();
}
return res;
```
Note that here we don't need to care about `String` x `String` and `StringName` x `StringName`
